he equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -36\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(-1)(\\frac{2}{3})+0(-36) \\\\ 0\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3})+2(-36)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -72\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535491 17592186045443 false] [17592186390985 1074 "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & ={e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+9\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -36\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -36\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -36\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(-1)(\\frac{2}{3})+0(-36) \\\\ 0\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3})+2(-36)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -72\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535491 17592186045443 true] [17592186390987 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 72\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535491 17592186045443 false] [17592186390987 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 72\\end{bmatrix}" 13194139535491 17592186045443 true] [17592186390988 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -72\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535