\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & ={e}^{-2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2(\\frac{1}{3})+0(6) \\\\ 0(\\frac{1}{3})+1(6)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535491 17592186045443 false] [17592186390992 1074 "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & ={e}^{-2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2(\\frac{1}{3})+0(6) \\\\ 0(\\frac{1}{3})+1(6)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535491 17592186045443 true] [17592186390994 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535491 17592186045443 false] [17592186390994 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}" 13194139535491 17592186045443 true] [17592186390995 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535491 17592186045443 false] [17592186390995 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}" 13194139535491 17592186045443 true] [17592186390996 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535491 17592186045443 false] [17592186390996 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" 13194139535491 17592186045443 true] [17592186390997 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535491 17592186045443 false] [17592186390997 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}" 13194139535491 17592186045443 true] [17592186390998 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535491 17592186045443 false] [17592186390998 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" 13194139535491 17592186045443 true] [17592186391043 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-7$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-7t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-7t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{-7t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/d6/d604781deed3206caf0a3bf6afa857c00beca9d532a26dedef5c717b4f684225.png)" 13194139535491 17592186045443 false] [17592186391043 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-7$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-7t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-7t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{-7t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/d6/d604781deed3206caf0a3bf6afa857c00beca9d532a26dedef5c717b4f684225.png)" 13194139535491 17592186045443 true] [17592186391050 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535491 17592186045443 false] [17592186391050 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139535491 17592186045443 true] [17592186391050 1074 "We have a decoupled system. Therefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{8t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-\\sqrt{5}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =(-3)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{8t}+6\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-\\sqrt{5}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{8t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-\\sqrt{5}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535491 17592186045443 false] [17592186391050 1074 "We have a decoupled system. Therefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{8t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-\\sqrt{5}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =(-3)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{8t}+6\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-\\sqrt{5}t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{8t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-\\sqrt{5}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535491 17592186045443 true] [17592186391052 1080 "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-\\sqrt{5}t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{8t}" 13194139535491 17592186045443 false] [17592186391052 1080 "x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-\\sqrt{5}t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{8t}" 13194139535491 17592186045443 true] [17592186391053 1080 "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{8t}" 13194139535491 17592186045443 false] [17592186391053 1080 "x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{8t}" 13194139535491 17592186045443 true] [17592186391054 1080 "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-\\sqrt{5}t}" 13194139535491 17592186045443 false] [17592186391054 1080 "x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-\\sqrt{5}t}" 13194139535491 17592186045443 true] [17592186391055 1080 "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{8t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-\\sqrt{5}t}" 13194139535491 17592186045443 false] [17592186391055 1080 "x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{8t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-\\sqrt{5}t}" 13194139535491 17592186045443 true] [17592186391056 1080 "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{8t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-\\sqrt{5}t}" 13194139535491 17592186045443 false] [17592186391056 1080 "x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{8