ral solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}={e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNotice that for each vector $\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n${e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ \n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/c4/c46568f3173dfeaab6e33e83269262e8692f13e245a42983ba1a9d4302ec5357.png)" 13194139535491 17592186045443 true] [17592186391194 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNotice that for each vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n${e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince ${e}^{-4t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the 